  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 205 PM CST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nThe impressive mid-latitude cyclone that brought widespread \nrain across the region will exit the region on Thursday, \nbringing an end to the precipitation. After that, expect dry \nconditions this weekend and most of next week. \n\nCURRENT TODAY - THURSDAY NIGHT\n\nEarly afternoon satellite and radar imagery showed a beautiful\noccluded system across the Upper Midwest, with the center of the\nupper level circulation along the Minnesota/Wisconsin border. A\nband of showers stretched from eastern South Dakota up through\nOntario. Low clouds blanketed the region along with additional\npockets of showers and drizzle.\n\nOvernight, the deformation band of precipitation will get\nsheared out as an upper level wave moves down from central\nCanada, and kick our system to the east on Thursday. This upper\nlevel wave will drive a large area of cold air across the Great\nLakes region, with H850 temps of -5 to -7C leading to banded\nlake effect precipitation Thursday evening and overnight. Did\ncontinue with chances for rain and snow across northern\nWisconsin, with accumulation of less than a half inch. The\n18Z HRRR is more aggressive with this precipitation, so if that\nsolution holds we could see perhaps an inch or two on grassy and\nelevated surfaces. On Thursday night, low temperatures will fall\ninto the 20s, with a few teens across northern Minnesota. \n\nFRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY\n\nHigh pressure will follow on Friday. For the weekend and into \nnext week, the storm track will be to the north of the \nInternational Border. The forecast is trending drier, as only a \nfew passing cold front will bring a wind shift and some mid \nlevel clouds. Temperatures will be on the cool side Friday and\nSaturday, but should moderate next week with highs back in the\n50s.",
